tor so for example if we were in Los  Angeles and we were looking at Bloods  and Crips what we would do is we would  teach what we call knowledge of self  classes oftentimes the african-american  community and the Latino community in  the United States does not ever get to  learn its history in US schools so we  begin by teaching young people what  their history is when they begin to know  who they are they have knowledge of self  they begin to have pride in who they are  then we begin to me what Maslow talked  about the hierarchy of needs if a young  person is hungry if they're homeless if  they're struggling how do we help them  meet their material needs in a safe way  in a way that no one has to be violent  so as material needs are being met as  they're learning their history then we  begin to look at our community and say  why does our community look this way why  are there so many liquor stores and very  few community centers what can we do  then we take an activist approach of  civic engagement and begin to organize  and about six months later we bring  these two rivals together where we do  something that we've been doing since  the beginning of time which is we break  bread we eat dinner together for the  first time rivals from different gangs  if we call it homies dinner and we begin  to learn we have a lot much more in  common than we do apart and that takes  time not everybody comes some people are  really scared of that we've brokered  relationships with nonprofits like the  YMCA here in the United States to be  able to do that work and it's been  powerful to see young people begin to  transform their lives and think of  themselves as warriors for change in a  peaceful way so what motivates you to  set this up I taught history for 15  years in California and every year I  went to more funerals than I ever went  to graduations every year that broke my  heart I felt like I was going up against  the stream and I just saw that the  problems of violence there's all kinds  of violence that I began to understand  is it violent when there are no food  outlets for people is it violent when  the level of poverty is intense in a  particular late neighborhood is it  violent when a community is flooded with  guns  and drugs and living in this  neighborhood living in Compton and  living in Oakland for 23 years I  realized we have to try something  different and we've tried gang  prevention for 60 years now name a gang  prevention program that has been  completely successful that doesn't exist  so what if gangs aren't just negative  what if they're not just evil  what if something good could come out of  gangs what if young people could take  care of their neighborhoods not just in  California but in London or in places  all over the world why should we  demonize young people why don't we try  to understand why they join these places  and that motivated me to begin and  through a lot of trial and error we  landed on the idea who doesn't want to  be around a space where there's love  support camaraderie and empowerment and  that's the recipe that we've been  cooking up in homies empowerment
 

